WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:11.060
So this week, we're going to cover two additional topics that are not really reflected in the

00:11.060 --> 00:17.980
final project, nor in the homework, nor obviously in the midterms.

00:17.980 --> 00:23.340
But just want to make sure that at least you have the high level concepts.

00:23.340 --> 00:29.080
Want to know that these concepts are there, and two, to guide you in terms of how to best

00:29.080 --> 00:35.240
choose future courses in case you're interested in getting deeper into this topic.

00:35.240 --> 00:40.060
In particular, today, we are going to talk about high level decision making.

00:40.060 --> 00:47.680
So not so much what exact path I should drive, but more like, is it a good time to cross

00:47.680 --> 00:50.760
an intersection or should I change a lane?

00:50.760 --> 00:55.800
And the problem is that when you try to reason about those high level decisions, accounting

00:55.800 --> 00:59.600
for uncertainty becomes pivotal.

00:59.600 --> 01:04.200
Because these decisions are affected by perceptual uncertainty, obviously, but also uncertainty

01:04.200 --> 01:05.720
in your environment reconstruction.

01:05.720 --> 01:11.440
For example, you have humans around you, you want to predict what they will do and take

01:11.440 --> 01:14.120
that prediction into account when you compute your actions.

01:14.120 --> 01:19.240
Of course, those predictions have a lot of uncertainty and you need to account for that

01:19.240 --> 01:24.060
in order to compute a high level action that trades safety versus efficiency.

01:24.060 --> 01:30.220
So the question is, what mathematical model we can use in order to capture decision making

01:30.220 --> 01:32.300
with uncertainty?

01:32.300 --> 01:36.320
And second of all, once we have a model, what type of algorithms are out there?

01:36.320 --> 01:42.120
So today we'll focus mostly on the mathematical model and the baseline algorithms.

01:42.120 --> 01:50.460
On Thursday, we'll focus on algorithms that are not really guaranteed to achieve optimality

01:50.460 --> 01:56.520
with respect to the model, but at least they are guaranteed to be very fast and very powerful.

01:56.520 --> 02:01.140
In other words, today we focus more on the topic of market decision processes and dynamic

02:01.140 --> 02:02.140
programming.

02:02.140 --> 02:05.920
On Thursday, we focus more on the topic of reinforcement learning, that from the perspective

02:05.920 --> 02:11.500
of this class is seen more as an approximate solution to market decision making problems

02:11.500 --> 02:15.340
than a standalone topic.

02:15.340 --> 02:21.780
So goal today is to learn the fundamental principles behind decision making and uncertainty

02:21.780 --> 02:28.420
specifically under a formulation in terms of market decision processes, and then discuss

02:28.420 --> 02:34.340
the workhorse algorithm that is used in order to tackle this problem referred to as dynamic

02:34.340 --> 02:35.340
programming.

02:35.340 --> 02:44.260
For this lecture, I'm going to reference a book from Dimitri Bertsikas, who was a professor

02:44.300 --> 02:45.300
at MIT.

02:45.300 --> 02:49.940
He wrote a book on reinforcement learning and optimal control.

02:49.940 --> 02:54.100
I think that if you want to get deeper into this material, this is an excellent book because

02:54.100 --> 03:01.140
basically it puts within the same unifying framework two fields that have been considered

03:01.140 --> 03:03.020
fairly separate.

03:03.020 --> 03:08.660
The field of optimal control, mostly in electrical engineering, aerospace engineering, and reinforcement

03:08.660 --> 03:10.780
learning, mostly in the field of computer science.

03:11.300 --> 03:13.100
Turns out that the foundations are the same.

03:13.100 --> 03:19.300
So it's very fruitful to actually reason about all these concepts together.

03:19.300 --> 03:27.460
So let's start right away with the mathematical model through which we capture a sequential

03:27.460 --> 03:28.780
decision making problem.

03:28.780 --> 03:35.060
So the idea is that over time, we want to make actions that are optimal according to

03:35.060 --> 03:37.580
some cost function.

03:37.580 --> 03:43.100
So how do we concretize this high-level description into a mathematical model?

03:43.100 --> 03:48.380
The idea is to set up a few basic ingredients.

03:48.380 --> 03:51.060
The first ingredient, we need a model.

03:51.060 --> 03:55.340
And this is very similar to our discussion in the context of Bayesian filtering.

03:55.340 --> 04:00.540
Here the main difference is that we are not just interested in estimating a process.

04:00.540 --> 04:05.420
We are interested in estimating a process and also controlling that process based on

04:05.420 --> 04:06.420
that estimate.

04:06.420 --> 04:08.700
So it's one step further, if you will.

04:08.700 --> 04:12.900
So the first ingredient, similar to what we have in the context of Bayesian filtering,

04:12.900 --> 04:15.660
we have a dynamics model.

04:15.660 --> 04:18.700
And for now, I'm considering a setting that is deterministic.

04:18.700 --> 04:20.300
So there is no uncertainty.

04:20.300 --> 04:23.660
We will consider later on the setting with stochasticity.

04:23.660 --> 04:26.900
But the main ideas actually can be understood in a deterministic setting.

04:26.900 --> 04:29.520
So I prefer to first cover the deterministic setting.

04:29.520 --> 04:45.480
So we have the dynamic system as k plus 1 equal to f of k x a u k.

04:45.480 --> 04:49.000
As usual, x is the state, u is the control.

04:49.000 --> 04:54.600
So what this is saying is that if at time k we are at state x of k and we apply control

04:54.600 --> 04:59.080
u of k, the next time step would be at state s k plus 1.

04:59.080 --> 05:00.960
So you already know this.

05:00.960 --> 05:03.640
As a second ingredient, we have control constraints.

05:03.640 --> 05:08.600
At each state, only a subset of the possible controls can be used.

05:08.600 --> 05:18.720
For example, you can know faster than a given speed because of speed rules on a rod segment.

05:18.720 --> 05:20.640
And we have a cost function.

05:20.640 --> 05:24.240
The cost function has an additive structure.

05:24.240 --> 05:30.280
And specifically, given initial condition, the cost function associated with a sequence

05:30.280 --> 05:36.040
of controls at time 0, time 1, all the way to time n minus 1 is equal to the sum of a

05:36.040 --> 05:40.760
final cost plus the summation of stage-wise costs.

05:40.760 --> 05:48.160
g k is the cost of being at state x of k and enacting control u of k at time k, a sum through

05:48.160 --> 05:53.920
all the time steps from time k equal 1 to the final time capital N minus 1.

05:53.920 --> 05:57.880
Capital N is the horizon of our optimization problem.

05:57.880 --> 06:00.760
g of n x n is a terminal cost.

06:00.760 --> 06:09.200
The problem then is to, given initial condition, to optimize the controls u k for each time

06:09.200 --> 06:14.400
step in such a way that the cost function is optimized.

06:14.400 --> 06:18.140
Now is this open loop or closed loop control?

06:18.140 --> 06:32.980
We had a discussion about open loop and closed loop in the setting of trajectory optimization.

06:32.980 --> 06:37.860
Because we are optimizing over a control sequence u 0, u 1, u n minus 1, which basically says

06:37.860 --> 06:47.220
that once you have computed your control sequence, u star k over k, so this is a set of controls,

06:47.220 --> 06:50.180
u 0, u 1, all the way to u n minus 1.

06:50.180 --> 06:57.260
Once you are at step k, whatever the state you are in, the control that you use is a

06:57.260 --> 07:01.200
u star k, no matter what the state you are in.

07:01.200 --> 07:06.620
So if the state you are in is actually the optimal state trajectory, that's good.

07:06.620 --> 07:07.940
It's nothing to do.

07:07.940 --> 07:12.180
But if for whatever reason, due, for example, to model mismatches, disturbances that are

07:12.180 --> 07:18.220
not accounted for here, we deviate from the nominal trajectory, then we apply a control

07:18.220 --> 07:19.780
that is completely nonsensical.

07:19.780 --> 07:28.220
So say that you are driving a car, and then you know that at 10 step, you have to use

07:28.220 --> 07:36.200
a speed 50 miles per hour, but for some reason, some tailwind allow you to go much faster.

07:36.200 --> 07:40.940
So actually you are much farther ahead on the road where you have a speed limit of 20

07:40.940 --> 07:41.940
miles per hour.

07:41.940 --> 07:45.860
You still apply 50 miles per hour as a control, because you don't measure your state.

07:45.860 --> 07:50.500
So that's why open loop control in general can be quite dangerous.

07:50.500 --> 07:59.060
But the point here is to set up the problem in such a simple framework, because the, oh,

07:59.060 --> 08:02.140
thank you, great.

08:02.140 --> 08:06.060
In these simple terms, because the main ideas can be understood within the simple framework,

08:06.060 --> 08:10.940
we will then make it more complicated by introducing stochasticity later on.

08:10.940 --> 08:11.940
So two key points.

08:11.940 --> 08:16.400
First of all, this is a discrete time model, which usually for high level decision making

08:16.400 --> 08:23.060
is good enough, since we have reason in terms of macro actions as opposed to a continuum

08:23.060 --> 08:25.940
of actions as it will be needed for trajectory optimization.

08:25.940 --> 08:29.620
And second of all, the cost is additive.

08:29.620 --> 08:33.560
So we have summing stage wise costs.

08:33.560 --> 08:34.820
So this is a central assumption.

08:34.820 --> 08:41.980
And we'll see why the assumption of the cost being additive is pivotal in this modeling

08:41.980 --> 08:45.540
approach from a computational standpoint in a second.

08:45.540 --> 08:54.760
In particular, the assumption of an additive cost allow us to dramatically simplify the

08:54.760 --> 09:01.100
problem of computing the optimal control sequence, which for a deterministic setup will still

09:01.100 --> 09:03.100
be possibly reasonable.

09:03.100 --> 09:09.660
But once we introduce the stochastic variation of this problem formulation, it will be impossible.

09:09.660 --> 09:11.460
Why is this problem difficult to solve?

09:11.460 --> 09:15.660
Because in general, J doesn't have any particular structure, so it's not complex.

09:15.660 --> 09:17.100
I mean, it might be.

09:17.100 --> 09:23.300
But in general, you have non-convex functions, the constraints will be highly non-linear.

09:23.300 --> 09:28.740
So solving this problem, at least in the most brutal way, would entail plugging in all possible

09:28.740 --> 09:34.820
combinations of values for u0, u1, un-1, which will correspond to a combinatorial explosion

09:34.820 --> 09:37.220
or things you have to evaluate.

09:37.220 --> 09:42.060
So the question is, can we leverage some structure in this problem formulation to make computation

09:42.060 --> 09:43.400
easier?

09:43.400 --> 09:48.100
So instead of doing like a brute force enumeration of all possible combinations controlled sequences,

09:48.100 --> 09:54.420
can we leverage some structure to actually avoid computation that for sure are not going

09:54.420 --> 09:57.580
to be fruitful, meaning leading to an optimal solution?

09:57.620 --> 10:00.340
So that's what the principal optimality does.

10:00.340 --> 10:03.780
And I'm going to introduce the principal optimality in a fairly high-level setting just to give

10:03.780 --> 10:06.220
you the main intuition.

10:06.220 --> 10:15.100
So the principal optimality is a key concept that allows us to devise the main workhorse

10:15.100 --> 10:19.860
algorithm behind the sequential decision-making, decision-making under uncertainty.

10:19.860 --> 10:22.660
Everything else falls from this.

10:22.660 --> 10:26.580
So you take like 15 classes in reinforcement learning here at Stanford, they're all rooted

10:26.580 --> 10:27.580
in this idea here.

10:27.580 --> 10:32.460
So I just want to make sure that the unifying ideas are clear to you.

10:32.460 --> 10:35.160
Now what does the principal optimality tell us?

10:35.160 --> 10:39.220
So first I'm going to introduce the notion and then we're going to discuss what is useful

10:39.220 --> 10:41.360
from a computational standpoint.

10:41.360 --> 10:45.400
So suppose that we have an optimal path.

10:45.400 --> 10:46.900
And this is just for conceptual purposes.

10:46.900 --> 10:51.140
As I said, our model is discrete, but let's assume that we reason in terms of trajectory

10:51.140 --> 10:53.300
optimization.

10:53.300 --> 10:57.820
So suppose that we have an optimal path that says that the optimal way to go from A to

10:57.820 --> 11:03.420
E is to first go from A to B and then from B to E along these paths.

11:03.420 --> 11:10.180
This is the cost to go from A to B and this is the cost to go from B to E. The cost would

11:10.180 --> 11:11.660
be some weird stuff.

11:11.660 --> 11:13.520
That's why we don't have a linear segment.

11:13.520 --> 11:16.900
We have these curved things.

11:16.900 --> 11:19.860
So this is an optimal trajectory.

11:19.940 --> 11:26.820
Now, as I said, the first decision allows you to go from A to B and incur cost JAB.

11:26.820 --> 11:35.180
And the remaining decisions allow you to go from B to E and incur cost GBE.

11:35.180 --> 11:40.340
Since the cost is additive, the optimal cost is simply the sum of this cost and the sum

11:40.340 --> 11:44.460
of the tail cost.

11:44.460 --> 11:53.080
Now, so what is the principle to mind this says?

11:53.080 --> 12:09.380
So assume that, so we have A, we have B, we have E, so we have JAB, JBB.

12:09.380 --> 12:18.780
So let's assume that the path A, B, E is optimal, OK?

12:18.780 --> 12:26.500
Now let's assume that for some reason you are parachuted at this location.

12:26.500 --> 12:30.660
So instead of starting from here, you're starting from here.

12:30.660 --> 12:36.460
The question is now you want to go from this point to this point optimally.

12:36.460 --> 12:43.420
The question is, in order to do so, can you just use the tail control sequence of the

12:43.420 --> 12:46.020
