12:46.020 --> 12:53.580
Or maybe there is some other route that is going to incur a cost that is less than the

12:53.580 --> 12:56.380
cost of JBE.

12:56.380 --> 12:57.380
It's a valid question.

12:57.380 --> 13:03.540
A priori, if I am parachuted into point B, there might be some other path that achieves

13:03.540 --> 13:06.680
lower cost.

13:06.680 --> 13:13.500
So the principle to mind this says that this is indeed not possible.

13:13.500 --> 13:21.740
So if you have an optimal trajectory, its tails are also optimal for the subcontinent.

13:21.740 --> 13:28.720
Meaning that if you are parachuted at B, it's still optimal to move along the trajectory

13:28.720 --> 13:33.500
that was the tail of the optimal sequence.

13:33.500 --> 13:34.740
Why that is the case?

13:34.740 --> 13:38.820
Well, let's prove this by contradiction.

13:38.820 --> 13:47.020
So assume that if you start at B, there is a drawing that is similar to the slide.

13:47.020 --> 13:52.460
Let's assume that if you start at B, there is another path that allows you to go to E

13:52.460 --> 13:54.140
with lower cost.

13:54.140 --> 14:01.820
For example, a path that goes to point C. By the way, oftentimes people say there is

14:01.820 --> 14:03.900
a more optimal path.

14:03.900 --> 14:06.560
More optimal is a nonsensical term.

14:06.560 --> 14:08.420
Something either is optimal or is not optimal.

14:08.420 --> 14:10.620
