44:21.300 --> 44:23.280
perspective of the trajectory.

44:23.280 --> 44:33.180
So this actual assumption allows you to prove principle of optimality concept that is very

44:33.180 --> 44:38.500
similar to the one that we proved for the deterministic setting.

44:38.500 --> 44:45.040
The objective is to find a closed loop policy before we were concerned with open loop policies.

44:45.040 --> 44:48.440
The cost is still additive, again, central assumption.

44:48.440 --> 44:50.800
And finally, we are considering a risk-neutral combination.

44:50.800 --> 44:53.820
So we want to optimize performance on average.

44:53.820 --> 44:58.900
Sometimes, as mentioned above, you're interested in going beyond average performance.

44:58.900 --> 45:04.420
We want to also count what happens in the worst case with respect to some quantile of

45:04.420 --> 45:06.420
your distribution and so on and so forth.

45:06.420 --> 45:12.420
This would lead to a risk-sensitive or risk-aware formulation of the problem.

45:12.420 --> 45:15.620
That is actually something that we work quite a bit in my lab, but is beyond the scope of

45:15.620 --> 45:16.620
this class.

45:16.620 --> 45:25.500
All right, as a note, the way that I stated the problem is based on a formalism that I

45:25.500 --> 45:28.060
really like, and it is rooted in control theory.

45:28.060 --> 45:32.860
If you go to a class in CS, you take, say, Emma Brunski's class on enforcement learning,

45:32.860 --> 45:34.500
her formalism will be different.

45:34.500 --> 45:38.260
So instead of a state transition equation, you have a probability kernel that tells you

45:38.260 --> 45:43.500
from state S. If you play action A, what is your next state S prime?

45:43.500 --> 45:44.500
Same thing.

45:44.500 --> 45:46.620
The only thing is that, unfortunately, different notations.

45:46.620 --> 45:51.620
Somehow the AI community, operational research community, and control theory community have

45:51.620 --> 45:54.320
developed a different notation.

45:54.320 --> 45:56.100
So what do you do when you have different languages?

45:56.100 --> 45:57.660
You have Rosetta Stone.

45:57.660 --> 46:01.980
So if you're confused about a notation, go into this paper.

46:01.980 --> 46:08.580
All this paper is about is to translate notation from one community to the other one.

46:08.580 --> 46:10.600
So it's a bit boring, but useful.

46:10.600 --> 46:13.380
Here you have F of X, A, U, K. No, wait.

46:13.380 --> 46:18.140
In AI, it's P, S, A, A, S prime, and so on and so forth.

46:18.140 --> 46:21.860
Seemed tedious, but actually it's quite useful.

46:21.860 --> 46:23.340
Did you know about this paper?

46:23.340 --> 46:25.340
You did, because you took two or three.

46:25.340 --> 46:26.340
Cool.

46:26.340 --> 46:27.340
All right.

46:27.900 --> 46:32.900
You can prove that even in the stochastic setting, and the proof is very, very similar

46:32.900 --> 46:38.540
to the proof I did before, the only complication is that now we had to reason in terms of these

46:38.540 --> 46:39.540
probability distributions.

46:39.540 --> 46:43.420
But you can prove a result that is almost identical.

46:43.420 --> 46:48.700
So assume that state S, K is reachable.

46:48.700 --> 46:52.620
Then consider this a problem whereby we are at X, K at time K, and we wish to minimize

46:52.620 --> 46:54.900
the cost to go from time K to N.

46:54.940 --> 47:00.180
Then the tail policy is optimal for the tail sub-problem.

47:00.180 --> 47:01.900
The same.

47:01.900 --> 47:08.260
Main difference here is A, we're talking about the tail policies as opposed to tail sequences,

47:08.260 --> 47:13.140
and two, the proof is slightly different because we have to account for probability distributions,

47:13.140 --> 47:15.140
but intuition is the same.

47:15.140 --> 47:18.420
As I said in this class, I want to just give you intuition.

47:18.420 --> 47:21.740
That's why I did it in a deterministic sense.

47:21.740 --> 47:26.260
The TPA algorithm itself is also extremely similar.

47:26.260 --> 47:27.940
You still proceed backward.

47:27.940 --> 47:34.260
You set the terminal cost to go equal to the terminal cost of your optimal control problem,

47:34.260 --> 47:38.100
and then go backward in time, you solve the Bellman equation.

47:38.100 --> 47:43.300
The only difference is that now you had to take an expectation with respect to the disturbance

47:43.300 --> 47:44.900
at the current time K.

47:44.900 --> 47:49.980
But the idea is the same, that in order to compute the optimal cost to go from each state,

47:51.780 --> 47:57.820
you compute the action that best trade-off immediate cost with the optimal cost to go.

48:03.900 --> 48:07.580
So let's do an example to make this a bit more concrete.

48:07.580 --> 48:13.780
An example is not really in the robotics field because I just want to distill the main ideas here.

48:13.780 --> 48:17.020
This is an inventory control problem.

48:17.020 --> 48:24.700
So the idea is that we have a warehouse whereby we can keep some stock and then we deliver stock.

48:24.700 --> 48:30.500
So in particular, we denote the amount of stock, which is an integer number,

48:30.500 --> 48:35.460
we can one unit, two units and some, as SOK, that's our state.

48:35.460 --> 48:44.540
The control, the inventory UK is the amount of stock that we buy trying to predict and meet future demands.

48:44.540 --> 48:47.580
And then we have a demand WK, which is a stochastic quantity,

48:47.580 --> 48:52.460
is the demand of that stock that we have each day.

48:52.460 --> 48:58.740
The dynamics are given by the maximum between zero and XK plus UK minus WK.

48:58.740 --> 49:06.460
What that means is that if I start with an amount of stock equal to X, I buy U and I get the demand W,

49:06.460 --> 49:13.140
then the stock at the next time is the difference between X plus U minus W.

49:13.140 --> 49:14.380
So please step forward.

49:14.380 --> 49:16.500
What I have the next time is the leftover.

49:16.500 --> 49:23.260
What I have after, I have shipped the stock that is required by the demand.

49:23.260 --> 49:32.700
The constraint is that the amount of stock that we can store at every single time can only be larger than two units.

49:32.700 --> 49:37.340
So X plus U has to be less than or equal to two.

49:37.340 --> 49:41.140
Of course, this is a very toy problem, but just to illustrate the main ideas.

49:41.180 --> 49:46.460
Finally, we have to prescribe a probabilistic structure for the disturbance,

49:46.460 --> 49:49.220
which in our case is the demand for the stock.

49:49.220 --> 49:52.620
And we say that we have three possible levels of demand.

49:52.620 --> 49:57.340
With probability 0.1, 10%, we have no demand at time K.

49:57.340 --> 50:00.580
Probability 70%, we have demand of one unit.

50:00.580 --> 50:04.660
Probability of 20%, we have demand for two units.

50:04.660 --> 50:08.980
This probability distribution in this case doesn't change through time, it's always the same.

50:11.500 --> 50:16.220
The cost is somewhat made up.

50:16.220 --> 50:23.020
And basically is a combination of the cost due to the fact that we are ordering new stock,

50:23.020 --> 50:26.580
and we say that the new stock costs $1, say,

50:26.580 --> 50:31.740
plus a cost due to the fact that we have to store material.

50:31.740 --> 50:34.500
Storing material incurs a cost.

50:34.500 --> 50:39.100
Maybe we have to pay workers to take care of the stock and so on.

50:39.140 --> 50:42.300
In particular, the cost is modeled as XK plus K minus WK.

50:42.300 --> 50:46.340
So that's the leftover stock that has to stay overnight.

50:46.340 --> 50:48.860
So we take the quantity, we square it.

50:48.860 --> 50:50.820
I mean, why not two, but why not two to the power of four?

50:50.820 --> 50:53.300
I mean, these will be made up to example.

50:54.660 --> 50:56.660
Well, so how do we solve this problem?

50:56.660 --> 51:02.140
How do we find a policy that tells you from each possible state the best thing to do?

51:02.140 --> 51:04.220
So how much stock you need to order?

51:04.220 --> 51:08.620
It's not trivial to really understand what would be optimal to do

51:08.620 --> 51:11.380
in terms of a control policy in this case.

51:11.380 --> 51:16.220
So in order to do so, what we do is to apply dynamic programming.

51:16.220 --> 51:23.700
So in particular, I forgot to mention that the terminal cost is zero.

51:23.700 --> 51:28.180
So if you're left with some stock, we just discard it, that incurs no cost.

51:28.180 --> 51:32.060
And the problem is over three time steps.

51:32.060 --> 51:37.420
Of course, the problem is very small just to solve it manually.

51:37.420 --> 51:39.460
Okay, so how do we solve this problem?

51:39.460 --> 51:44.660
This problem has all the ingredients of a Markov decision process.

51:44.660 --> 51:47.620
So basically, the model that I described before.

51:47.620 --> 51:49.700
Cost is additive.

51:49.700 --> 51:54.060
Remember that additive does not mean that the immediate costs are linear function.

51:54.060 --> 51:58.060
It means that I can sum immediate costs through time.

51:58.060 --> 52:00.500
The probability distribution is Markovian.

52:00.500 --> 52:04.540
We have all the ingredients, so we can apply dynamic programming.

52:04.540 --> 52:09.540
So the first step would be to say that the terminal cost to go

52:09.540 --> 52:12.340
is equal to the terminal cost.

52:12.340 --> 52:19.340
In this case, J3 of X3 is equal to zero.

52:19.340 --> 52:27.020
Because remember that the terminal cost is by problem definition equal to zero.

52:27.020 --> 52:32.940
So now, how do we compute the optimal control policy?

52:32.940 --> 52:35.420
Well, we have to carry out the dynamic programming algorithm.

52:35.420 --> 52:36.980
So we go one step backward.

52:36.980 --> 52:39.380
So from three, we go to two.

52:39.380 --> 52:42.620
And we compute the optimal cost to go function

52:42.620 --> 52:46.660
for each possible value of the state.

52:46.660 --> 52:51.980
What are the possible values of the state?

52:51.980 --> 52:53.780
Remember that we have this constraint here,

52:53.780 --> 53:00.420
which means that X can either be equal to zero, one or two.

53:00.420 --> 53:06.820
So we have to compute J20, J21, and J22.

53:06.820 --> 53:10.460
Once I've done these three computations, then I go backward in time.

53:10.460 --> 53:13.340
I either compute J1 for all possible values of the states,

53:13.340 --> 53:17.020
and then I go back to J0, and then I've solved the problem.

53:17.020 --> 53:22.740
So let's compute J20 just to do an example.

53:22.740 --> 53:26.380
So the J20, according to the Bellman equation,

53:26.380 --> 53:33.660
is the minimum over the feasible controls.

53:33.660 --> 53:35.860
So what are the feasible controls here?

53:35.860 --> 53:38.020
We go back to the constraint.

53:38.020 --> 53:42.460
So the constraint says that X plus U has to be less than or equal to two.

53:42.460 --> 53:47.060
We are considering as an initial state for the Taylor's problem zero,

53:47.060 --> 53:51.780
which means that zero plus U has to be less than or equal to two,

53:51.780 --> 53:55.860
which means that U can be either zero, one or two.

53:55.900 --> 53:57.540
Make sense?

53:57.540 --> 54:05.620
So this is the minimum over U2 equal either zero, one or two

54:05.620 --> 54:12.580
of the expected value of the immediate cost.

54:12.580 --> 54:18.660
So U2 plus zero, which is the value of the state,

54:18.660 --> 54:27.340
plus U2 minus W2, plus the cost to go

54:27.340 --> 54:30.340
from the perspective of the following state,

54:30.340 --> 54:33.980
which we have already computed, so this is equal to zero.

54:33.980 --> 54:35.980
And here I forgot the square.

54:38.580 --> 54:41.580
All right, so let's do this computation.

54:48.660 --> 55:01.140
All right, so this is equal to...

55:07.140 --> 55:11.020
equal to, we have to compute the expectation.

55:11.020 --> 55:14.940
Expectation means that we take the outcome times the probability of that outcome.

55:14.940 --> 55:16.420
So what is the random variable?

55:16.420 --> 55:18.100
It is W2.

55:18.100 --> 55:24.580
So with probability equal to 0.1.

55:28.580 --> 55:31.100
I'm balanced by this one.

55:33.780 --> 55:37.540
So the expectation, so U2 does not depend on any stochasticity.

55:37.540 --> 55:38.780
So we can bring it out.

55:38.780 --> 55:44.700
So it is equal to U2 plus, so this is the stochastic quantity.

55:44.700 --> 55:50.380
So with probability equal to 10%, W takes on the value of zero.

55:50.380 --> 55:54.620
So we have...

55:54.620 --> 55:59.820
So minimum over U2 equals zero, one or two of U2

55:59.820 --> 56:06.220
plus U2 minus zero square, probability 10%,

56:06.220 --> 56:14.660
plus plus U2 minus one square, probability 70%.

56:15.660 --> 56:22.660
Plus U2 minus two square, probability 20%.

56:29.660 --> 56:33.300
U2 minus two, all right.

56:33.300 --> 56:36.820
So then what you do is to plug in this expression,

56:36.820 --> 56:40.700
either U2 equals zero, U2 equals one, or U2 equals two.

56:40.700 --> 56:43.460
You evaluate it, and then you take the minimum.

56:43.500 --> 56:50.180
In this case, the minimum is obtained for U2 equal one.

56:50.180 --> 56:55.420
The cost in that case, you could check.

56:55.420 --> 56:58.500
Is J2 equal 1.3?

56:58.500 --> 57:04.020
And then we set the value of the policy at time two

57:04.020 --> 57:07.220
from state zero as equal one, which

57:07.220 --> 57:11.500
means that at state zero, at time step two,

57:11.500 --> 57:14.060
we had to choose control one.

57:14.060 --> 57:19.820
Then I will solve G21, J22, same reasoning,

57:19.820 --> 57:21.780
and then we'll go one step backward.

57:21.780 --> 57:24.100
And then if you do that, you should do it as an exercise

57:24.100 --> 57:27.300
that you will get.

57:27.300 --> 57:29.420
OK, so any questions here?

57:35.020 --> 57:37.500
This is really how you solve stochastic sequential decision

57:37.500 --> 57:39.140
making problems.

57:39.140 --> 57:44.260
Everything else is basically a variation on this idea.

57:44.260 --> 57:46.500
In particular, why do we need variations?

57:46.500 --> 57:48.700
The problem is that this problem is coarse.

57:48.700 --> 57:51.620
And usually the carceral dimensionality

57:51.620 --> 57:53.420
is the only one that is mentioned,

57:53.420 --> 57:56.020
but in reality, you have many more courses.

57:56.020 --> 57:58.300
So first of all, you have the carceral dimensionality.

57:58.300 --> 58:00.340
So what is the carceral dimensionality?

58:00.340 --> 58:02.340
The problem is that even the dynamic programming

58:02.340 --> 58:06.420
allow you to do very streamlined computation,

58:06.420 --> 58:08.620
reusing all the information from the solution

58:08.620 --> 58:10.020
to the tails-up problem.

58:10.020 --> 58:13.780
Still, at each time step, you have

58:13.780 --> 58:20.060
to solve the Bellman equation, which is this thing here,

58:20.060 --> 58:22.940
for each possible value of the state.

58:22.940 --> 58:26.620
So if the state is a one-dimensional object that

58:26.620 --> 58:29.460
can take on 100 values, it's fine.

58:29.460 --> 58:34.140
But assume that the state is now a three-dimensional object,

58:34.140 --> 58:36.220
x1, x2, x3.

58:36.220 --> 58:40.620
So you have to take all the combination of x1, x2, and x3.

58:40.620 --> 58:44.100
So if you have 10 values, how many combinations you have?

58:49.460 --> 58:51.540
So basically, this grows exponentially

58:51.540 --> 58:53.980
with the dimension, which means that you go on five,

58:53.980 --> 58:57.660
six dimensions, you are hopeless.

58:57.660 --> 59:00.660
On top of it, and that's what you were asking,

59:00.660 --> 59:05.940
if a sum of the states cannot be observed directly,

59:05.940 --> 59:08.420
what you do in order to be in this formalism

59:08.420 --> 59:13.140
is to build a probability distribution around it, which

59:13.140 --> 59:14.580
means, though, that you can still

59:14.580 --> 59:16.180
use the dynamic programming principle,

59:16.180 --> 59:19.060
but now you have to reason in terms of beliefs, which

59:19.060 --> 59:22.460
means that now your optimal cost to go

59:22.460 --> 59:26.540
is not related to the state, but related to the belief

59:26.540 --> 59:28.020
of that state, which means that you

59:28.020 --> 59:32.220
have to discretize or approximate

59:32.220 --> 59:34.940
a probability distribution to run this Bellman equation.

59:34.940 --> 59:37.100
So computationally, very complicated.

59:37.100 --> 59:40.620
This will lead to the so-called POMDP problems.

59:40.620 --> 59:43.500
Similarly, curves of modeling.

59:43.500 --> 59:52.460
Oftentimes, it's very complex to get close-form expressions

59:52.460 --> 59:54.980
for these probability distributions.

59:54.980 --> 59:58.500
So sometimes, well, yeah, this one's here.

59:58.500 --> 01:00:01.940
Sometimes, it will be possible to compute them,

01:00:01.940 --> 01:00:03.980
but they're extremely complicated.

01:00:04.020 --> 01:00:06.260
Or sometimes, they're not really known.

01:00:06.260 --> 01:00:08.700
For example, if you are trying to model

01:00:08.700 --> 01:00:12.780
how a human would react to your actions, that, of course,

01:00:12.780 --> 01:00:16.340
is an influence in what the next state will be.

01:00:16.340 --> 01:00:20.260
But who is going to give you that information?

01:00:20.260 --> 01:00:21.860
So you have to learn that from data.

01:00:21.860 --> 01:00:23.780
So that's so-called curves of modeling,

01:00:23.780 --> 01:00:26.260
that some of the problem formulation

01:00:26.260 --> 01:00:29.900
may not be given to you, and yet you want to solve this problem.

01:00:29.900 --> 01:00:31.700
Finally, curves of time.

01:00:31.700 --> 01:00:34.700
Oftentimes, the data of the problem

01:00:34.700 --> 01:00:37.140
is given to you with little advanced notice.

01:00:37.140 --> 01:00:40.540
For example, in the context of inventory control

01:00:40.540 --> 01:00:43.740
or dispatching, scheduling, so not so much in robotics.

01:00:43.740 --> 01:00:46.980
In the context of robotics, the problem data

01:00:46.980 --> 01:00:50.300
may change as the system is controlled,

01:00:50.300 --> 01:00:52.860
which means that you have to recompute these policies

01:00:52.860 --> 01:00:56.860
online, possibly by recomputing some parts of the problem

01:00:56.860 --> 01:00:59.740
formulation online, by adapting, for example,

01:00:59.780 --> 01:01:04.460
the transition model to the model that reflects

01:01:04.460 --> 01:01:07.500
the current capabilities of the robot.

01:01:07.500 --> 01:01:09.900
So this has triggered a lot of interest

01:01:09.900 --> 01:01:15.780
in suboptimal dynamic programming, which you say,

01:01:15.780 --> 01:01:16.540
boring.

01:01:16.540 --> 01:01:18.780
Let's say exciting reinforcement learning.

01:01:18.780 --> 01:01:21.700
OK, so for me, it's suboptimal dynamic programming.

01:01:21.700 --> 01:01:24.460
So basically, the idea is to come up

01:01:24.460 --> 01:01:28.660
with approximations that make the dynamic programming

01:01:28.700 --> 01:01:32.340
algorithm scalable to a number of different settings.

01:01:32.340 --> 01:01:34.380
High dimensional spaces, situations

01:01:34.380 --> 01:01:36.780
whereby parts of the model are not specified,

01:01:36.780 --> 01:01:38.180
they had to be learned from data,

01:01:38.180 --> 01:01:40.500
they're offline, online, and so on.

01:01:40.500 --> 01:01:43.380
In particular, and this will be the subject of the lecture

01:01:43.380 --> 01:01:46.940
on Thursday, there are basically two main ideas

01:01:46.940 --> 01:01:52.380
behind suboptimal DP, or if you will, enforcement learning.

01:01:52.380 --> 01:01:54.020
The idea is to either approximate

01:01:54.020 --> 01:01:56.620
the problem in a value space, whereby

01:01:56.620 --> 01:01:59.300
we approximate the optimal cost-to-go function

01:01:59.300 --> 01:02:03.340
with some surrogate, or approximate the problem

01:02:03.340 --> 01:02:06.580
in policy space, whereby we select the policy

01:02:06.580 --> 01:02:11.420
from a restricted class of policies.

01:02:11.420 --> 01:02:13.460
Let me just give you, in the final 10 minutes,

01:02:13.460 --> 01:02:16.180
the intuition between these two classes, which, of course,

01:02:16.180 --> 01:02:18.580
has been moving in several different modeling

01:02:18.580 --> 01:02:20.500
approaches, algorithms, and this is

01:02:20.500 --> 01:02:22.540
a very active field of research.

01:02:22.540 --> 01:02:29.860
So the first approach is to, OK, solving this problem

01:02:29.860 --> 01:02:33.860
allows you to solve all the tail subproblems, which

01:02:33.860 --> 01:02:39.860
is very complicated, difficult, and most of the time

01:02:39.860 --> 01:02:42.060
is intractable.

01:02:42.060 --> 01:02:46.740
So the idea is that if you want to compute a policy at,

01:02:46.740 --> 01:02:49.220
actually, your action at time k, you still

01:02:49.220 --> 01:02:51.020
use the idea of a dynamic programming.

01:02:51.060 --> 01:02:54.500
So you compute a policy that best trades off

01:02:54.500 --> 01:02:56.740
immediate cost with the cost-to-go.

01:02:56.740 --> 01:02:59.260
But for the cost-to-go, you don't use the optimal one,

01:02:59.260 --> 01:03:01.340
because maybe it's too computationally intensive

01:03:01.340 --> 01:03:04.380
to compute it, or you don't have enough problem information

01:03:04.380 --> 01:03:09.100
to compute it, but you use a surrogate cost-to-go.

01:03:09.100 --> 01:03:12.700
So something that hopefully is close to the optimal cost-to-go.

01:03:12.700 --> 01:03:15.700
Well, OK, so the $100 question is,

01:03:15.700 --> 01:03:17.460
how do you compute this surrogate cost-to-go

01:03:17.460 --> 01:03:19.420
that still allows you to compute an action that

01:03:20.020 --> 01:03:21.060
good enough?

01:03:21.060 --> 01:03:22.700
Well, there are many different classes.

01:03:22.700 --> 01:03:25.380
Within each class, you have many different subclasses.

01:03:25.380 --> 01:03:28.500
But you can do, for example, problem approximation.

01:03:28.500 --> 01:03:30.620
So to compute the optimal cost-to-go,

01:03:30.620 --> 01:03:32.940
you consider a problem that is simpler

01:03:32.940 --> 01:03:36.140
but has some commonality to the problem you're trying to solve.

01:03:36.140 --> 01:03:38.140
In the case of motion planning, say

01:03:38.140 --> 01:03:43.060
that you're computing a trajectory for your robot,

01:03:43.060 --> 01:03:44.780
and the cost-to-go is the solution

01:03:44.780 --> 01:03:47.660
to the optimal trajectory from some state in the future.

01:03:47.660 --> 01:03:49.420
For that problem, instead of using

01:03:49.420 --> 01:03:51.700
the full dynamics of the robot, you

01:03:51.700 --> 01:03:53.580
consider simplified dynamics in order

01:03:53.580 --> 01:03:57.900
to compute the cost-to-go, for example.

01:03:57.900 --> 01:04:02.580
Second approach is to do approximate optimization.

01:04:02.580 --> 01:04:04.780
A third approach that is very popular

01:04:04.780 --> 01:04:09.140
is to approximate the cost-to-go with a functional approximator,

01:04:09.140 --> 01:04:11.260
such as a neural network, and then learn

01:04:11.260 --> 01:04:13.940
this approximation from data.

01:04:13.940 --> 01:04:17.020
And then, of course, there are zillions of ways of doing it.

01:04:17.060 --> 01:04:18.940
And entire classes devoted to it.

01:04:18.940 --> 01:04:20.860
And then state aggregation.

01:04:20.860 --> 01:04:24.300
This is oftentimes not mentioned in classes.

01:04:24.300 --> 01:04:26.900
But the idea is, instead of reasoning

01:04:26.900 --> 01:04:29.380
in terms of individual states, you

01:04:29.380 --> 01:04:31.940
reason in terms of macro states, so that you

01:04:31.940 --> 01:04:34.340
reduce the dimensionality of the state space.

01:04:34.340 --> 01:04:38.260
With the idea that, OK, you don't know anymore

01:04:38.260 --> 01:04:41.620
what you have to do if you are exactly at a given

01:04:41.620 --> 01:04:42.660
latitude and longitude.

01:04:42.660 --> 01:04:46.220
But if you are within a circle on a given latitude and longitude,

01:04:46.220 --> 01:04:50.100
you know more or less what to do that is good enough.

01:04:50.100 --> 01:04:51.700
So the other approach, as I mentioned,

01:04:51.700 --> 01:04:53.580
is approximation in policy space.

01:04:53.580 --> 01:04:58.500
So I suppose, when we do policy optimization,

01:04:58.500 --> 01:05:00.500
so when we tend to compute the optimum policy,

01:05:00.500 --> 01:05:02.940
we are not putting any restriction in the policies

01:05:02.940 --> 01:05:06.700
we are computing, which makes the problem very complicated.

01:05:06.700 --> 01:05:09.260
So a popular approach is basically

01:05:09.260 --> 01:05:12.380
consider a parameterized class of a policy.

01:05:12.420 --> 01:05:17.540
So basically, functions that depends on some parameter rk.

01:05:17.540 --> 01:05:22.060
And then by using, for example, data, so in a learning context

01:05:22.060 --> 01:05:27.460
or some other technique, you optimize over those parameters.

01:05:27.460 --> 01:05:29.660
So what's the main difference is that you are transforming

01:05:29.660 --> 01:05:32.140
a problem from an optimization of the space of functions

01:05:32.140 --> 01:05:35.060
over a problem in terms of optimization over parameters.

01:05:35.060 --> 01:05:37.220
So finite dimensional objects.

01:05:37.220 --> 01:05:39.740
So what we do on Thursday will be

01:05:39.740 --> 01:05:42.660
to go deeper into these two approaches

01:05:42.660 --> 01:05:47.180
to scale up stochastic optimization

01:05:47.180 --> 01:05:49.020
and dynamic programming to problems

01:05:49.020 --> 01:05:52.780
where either the state space is too large

01:05:52.780 --> 01:05:56.660
or some of the problem data is not available

01:05:56.660 --> 01:05:59.980
and have to be learned online.

01:05:59.980 --> 01:06:01.540
Which means that the topic of Thursday

01:06:01.540 --> 01:06:03.860
will be reinforcement learning.

01:06:03.860 --> 01:06:07.020
Now, so as I said, this is the final week.

01:06:07.020 --> 01:06:11.020
So Friday, we'll have the final project demo.

01:06:11.020 --> 01:06:16.060
So I hope that you're having fun with the final project.

