01:06:20.060 --> 01:06:22.660
in your course evaluations.

01:06:22.660 --> 01:06:26.380
This, I think, one of the most difficult classes to teach.

01:06:26.380 --> 01:06:28.740
Definitely the most difficult class to teach,

01:06:28.740 --> 01:06:30.300
that I teach here at Stanford.

01:06:30.300 --> 01:06:34.820
Because material per se is rather sophisticated,

01:06:34.820 --> 01:06:37.020
but also has a very strong software component.

01:06:37.020 --> 01:06:39.980
So you're not just writing a script.

01:06:39.980 --> 01:06:42.940
You are interacting with a complicated software

01:06:42.940 --> 01:06:45.020
infrastructure, which I think is a very good learning

01:06:45.020 --> 01:06:45.940
opportunity.

01:06:45.940 --> 01:06:47.980
But of course, it's difficult to establish.

01:06:47.980 --> 01:06:50.820
Plus, you have also hands-on hardware.

01:06:50.820 --> 01:06:53.860
So every year, I really take into serious account

01:06:53.860 --> 01:06:56.020
the feedback that I receive from your colleagues

01:06:56.020 --> 01:06:57.660
in order to improve this course.

01:06:57.660 --> 01:06:59.820
This course basically changes every year

01:06:59.820 --> 01:07:02.500
by taking into account the feedback from the previous years.

01:07:02.500 --> 01:07:04.500
All this to say that I really value your feedback.

01:07:04.500 --> 01:07:08.260
So try to put some time in providing feedback

01:07:08.260 --> 01:07:11.020
